um share some of our work in this area and i&nbsp;
do wish i could be there in person for all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:28.160
these different activities but um until then the&nbsp;
virtual format will have to do so um what i'd like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:34.720
to share with you today is just some some thoughts&nbsp;
and some data about the topic of neuroinformatics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.160
and cognitive ontologies so for those of us&nbsp;
working in cognitive neuroscience we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.680
there have been decades and decades of fmri&nbsp;
functional magnetic resonance imaging work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.840
that's identified distinct patterns of brain&nbsp;
activation that occur while participants perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.200
different kinds of cognitive tests and the goal of&nbsp;
course is to map the areas of the brain that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.640
involved in different types of cognitive processes&nbsp;
um sort of the goal of cognitive neuroscience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.960
very broadly and what we would like to suggest&nbsp;
here today is that these data can in principle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:15.600
be used to provide the basis for constructing&nbsp;
a biologically informed data-driven ontology or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.960
taxonomy of psychological processes and there's&nbsp;
many others who've worked in this area too over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.960
the years and i'll draw on some of that literature&nbsp;
as well so in this talk i'd like to highlight some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.560
of the progress and challenges associated with&nbsp;
the construction of cognitive ontologies using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:43.120
informatics approaches and based on functional&nbsp;
neuroimaging data so let me go the right direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:52.080
let's see okay so what is a cognitive ontology um&nbsp;
so an ontology generally is a branch of philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.240
that deals with the nature and organization&nbsp;
of reality so what things are there in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.440
world out there how do we carve up nature at its&nbsp;
joints that's really what ontology deals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.240
um and so a cognitive ontology then would&nbsp;
be a formal description of concepts that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.000
assumed to exist within a particular domain&nbsp;
here we're talking about psychology and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.720
interrelationships between them so uh one sort of&nbsp;
easy way of thinking about ontologies is just what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.320
are the knowledge structures that we you know know&nbsp;
about uh that we use to communicate amongst each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.560
other so you know we just saw the the nba finals&nbsp;
with the the lakers uh coming out as champs again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.400
um and if you think about an ontology of the nba&nbsp;
you have things like basic units like players that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.960
belong to teams and you have a western conference&nbsp;
in an eastern conference and knowing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.520
knowledge structure tells you that in the finals&nbsp;
you're going to have a team from the west play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.960
a team from the east because that's the structure&nbsp;
of the nba and knowing all the things we do about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.680
teams and their compositions and the players&nbsp;
that make them up it tells us something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.920
what we can expect um in any uh you know given&nbsp;
category to contain so just knowledge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.120
way the nba works is a particular ontology and&nbsp;
just like that we we had these sort of knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.800
structures in in various domains so you know in&nbsp;
the case of of cognitive ontology we might uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.720
think about something like a particular task&nbsp;
um and how we should categorize that so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:27.360
people use these simple task paradigms the flanker&nbsp;
task is one in which the participant is asked look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.320
at the arrow in the center and tell me if it's&nbsp;
pointing to the left or pointing to the right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.960
you use a button press to indicate your response&nbsp;
and the challenge of this task is sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:42.480
there's flankers to that central arrow going in&nbsp;
opposite direction and um you know anytime you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.040
do this behavioral paradigm it slows people down&nbsp;
when there are arrows uh pointing in the opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.160
direction of the center arrow this task has been&nbsp;
around for a long time and we use it um you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:57.680
in the laboratory uh and the question is what&nbsp;
what are we using this task for what process what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.880
cognitive process do we think we're tapping when&nbsp;
we do a task like this and a cognitive ontology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.480
the research in this area would actually formalize&nbsp;
this kind of question so if you had for example

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:18.880
so if you had one individual who says well this&nbsp;
is a response inhibition test that's what we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.360
measuring here the cognitive construct of response&nbsp;
inhibition and then you might have another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.320
scientist say no what we're really looking at when&nbsp;
we ask people to do this is error detection that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.560
what i think the flanker task is mapping and in&nbsp;
what we do when we look at cognitive ontology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.960
research is formalize this type of disagreement&nbsp;
into a research program where um you know we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.160
say well let's figure out what it what cognitive&nbsp;
construct or process is this task really tapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.720
and how do we get to the answer to that question&nbsp;
uh what what is a way to sort of uh create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:59.120
better cognitive ontology or categorization of&nbsp;
these mental and uh psychological constructs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.080
so there's several approaches for constructing&nbsp;
cognitive ontologies and they can sort of broadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:12.240
fall into the category of uh bottom up top bottom&nbsp;
up and top down so a top-down approach is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.960
you would use expert knowledge and opinion&nbsp;
to um you know get a group of researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.760
to agree well when you do this task what you're&nbsp;
really engaging is this cognitive construct and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.640
now we all agree on that and we can write it&nbsp;
down and use it to communicate um so there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.000
been a lot of work in this area um using&nbsp;
expert opinion and topic modeling of text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.520
in article databases you'll see some of the&nbsp;
references here russ podrick's group ng laird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:43.680
jessica turner italia arcone have done a lot of&nbsp;
this work building a top-down sort of cognitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.480
ontology and of course the benefit to something&nbsp;
like that is that you can you know produce a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:55.360
detailed ontology of relationships and entities&nbsp;
but there is of course the downside that this may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.760
fail to map onto actual divisions or categories&nbsp;
of nature and uh and there's some other downsides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.800
that i'll mention in in a minute but um this has&nbsp;
been one sort of popular approach for construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.480
of a cognitive ontology and on the other hand&nbsp;
you have this bottom-up approaches it's usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.560
a data-driven or more informatics-based approach&nbsp;
so data-driven classification or clustering of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:26.080
functional imaging or behavioral data and you&nbsp;
can also you know use supervised machine learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:33.680
for this type of thing or exploratory unsupervised&nbsp;
machine learning um and uh this this bottom-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:40.480
approach basically um you know can try to derive&nbsp;
cognitive processed categories of cognitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:48.080
processes directly from task fmri patterns and the&nbsp;
pros of this is that this an ontology derived in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.560
this manner might more likely reflect actual&nbsp;
divisions or categories of nature that we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.520
but they may not necessarily provide enough detail&nbsp;
to be a practical use for knowledge representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:06.000
so um you know every sort of approach has&nbsp;
has its pros and cons so one example just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.040
you know that many people might be familiar with&nbsp;
is of a top-down approach is this research domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:17.440
criteria that the national institute of health&nbsp;
has um sort of put forth and this is a way uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.800
it's a sort of a call for new ways of classifying&nbsp;
mental illness that are based on dimensions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:29.280
observable behavior and neurobiological measures&nbsp;
um and other measures and this is uh just a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:35.440
um kind of summary of some of the categories&nbsp;
within the research domain criteria uh back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:41.040
in 2016 i was part of this cognitive group&nbsp;
the blue group here in the middle um that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:47.040
basically it was a group of experts who decided&nbsp;
what the categories were that um would be of use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:53.600
for study um you know to standardize across uh&nbsp;
psychiatric disorders and uh to push forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:59.920
uh personalized medicine and so forth um and being&nbsp;
on the panel of you know on this top down panel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:04.560
made me realize sort of firsthand what were&nbsp;
some of the downsides of doing the top-down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.320
approach and one of them is of course everyone who&nbsp;
studies uh cognitive neuroscience has their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.920
biases and their own sort of lifetime of of ways&nbsp;
of thinking about things and so expert based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.840
approaches are prone to the kinds of biases that&nbsp;
experts have so you may be a person who studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.280
executive function so you think these particular&nbsp;
aspects of cognition are the most important or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.360
the most salient or the most interesting for&nbsp;
investigation doesn't necessarily mean that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.400
the way the brain is carved up or that's the way&nbsp;
that the mind works but um so you know that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:43.840
was a top-down kind of ontological approach um&nbsp;
but the reason that this sort of research domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:50.560
criteria has been so popular is that the goal was&nbsp;
to carve up psychiatric disorders in a way that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:56.000
not just symptom-based so it's not just you see&nbsp;
a person with observable symptoms in this manner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.520
and you put them into major depression or bipolar&nbsp;
or some other category because you know of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:07.360
symptoms themselves cannot tell you much about&nbsp;
the underlying mechanism so perhaps if you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:14.080
genetic risks brain activity physiology behavior&nbsp;
and life experience and derived data-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:19.520
clusters of individuals who were more similar&nbsp;
within cluster than a cross-cluster um those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:24.160
those people might not all fall into the category&nbsp;
of major depressive disorder but they might all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:31.120
be most uh well treated by a particular type&nbsp;
of of drug or intervention because once you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:37.040
identified clusters that are more homogeneous the&nbsp;
treatment and kind of response research can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:43.840
more targeted and hopefully more uh efficient so&nbsp;
rdoc has really prompted individuals in the field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:49.440
of psychiatry to rethink how psychopathology&nbsp;
might be best served by building a framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:56.080
that's based more on biological and cognitive&nbsp;
validity so you know a lot of people working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.360
with the rdoc framework now look for research&nbsp;
findings that cut across diagnostic boundaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.680
and uh you know this is all towards the sort of&nbsp;
goal of precision medicine so you can see why you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.240
know despite there being flaws in the top-down&nbsp;
approach there there's been a lot of success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:19.120
with this uh method and um there's been a lot of&nbsp;
work uh this was from russ paul drake and talia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:25.600
irconi trying to derive things like cognitive&nbsp;
atlases which carve up the space of uh you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

